      n: 1, title: 'No. 1 — General Introduction (Hamilton)', tourTitle: 'No. 1: Hamilton Sets the Stakes',
      hook: 'Hamilton opens the argument: America\'s choice between good government by reflection and chance, or forever condemned to accident and force.',
      blurb: 'Hamilton\'s overture: can mankind establish good government by deliberate choice, or are we forever condemned to accident and force? The question, he says, is not just America\'s — it is the world\'s.',
      summary: [
        'Hamilton opens by naming what is at stake: the question put to America is whether societies of men are capable of establishing good government through reflection and choice, or whether they must forever depend on accident and force. The stakes, he says, extend beyond America — the outcome will be of consequence to the happiness of nations yet unborn. If the Union fails to ratify a workable constitution, he implies, it will deal a blow to the prospects of republican government everywhere. This opening move — framing a domestic ratification argument as a question of universal significance — is characteristic of The Federalist\'s rhetorical ambition.',
        'Hamilton then does something unusual for a pamphleteer: he asks the reader to distrust his own arguments alongside the opposition\'s. Those who oppose the Constitution will cry tyranny; some will be sincere and mistaken, others motivated by self-interest — officeholders who stand to lose power under a stronger federal government, or ambitious men who see opportunity in disorder. But Hamilton is equally candid that supporters of the Constitution may have their own biases. What follows, he says, will be an argument, not a tract: judge the reasoning, not the man making it. The reader is invited to exercise independent judgment throughout.',
        'Hamilton closes by laying out the plan: eighty-five papers will cover the utility of union to political prosperity, the insufficiency of the existing Confederation, the necessity of a government at least as energetic as the one proposed, the conformity of the Constitution to republican principles, the analogy between the Constitution and the various state constitutions, and the additional security the Constitution would provide to liberty and property. This table of contents serves as the reader\'s map through what follows — each subsequent paper picks up one thread of this outline and develops it in argument and evidence.',
      ],
